# Step 1 — Create VPC

Open:

```
VPC Dashboard → Your VPCs → Create VPC
```

Choose:

```
VPC only
```

Fill:

| Field | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Name | MyVPC |
| IPv4 CIDR | 10.0.0.0/16 |

Click:

```
Create VPC
```

---

# Step 2 — Create Subnet

Go to:

```
Subnets → Create subnet
```

Select your VPC:

```
MyVPC
```

Fill:

| Field | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Subnet name | PublicSubnet |
| Availability Zone | any |
| IPv4 CIDR | 10.0.1.0/24 |

Click:

```
Create subnet
```

---

# Step 3 — Enable Auto Public IP

Open:

```
Subnets → Select PublicSubnet
```

Click:

```
Actions → Edit subnet settings
```

Enable:

```
Auto-assign public IPv4 address
```

Save.

This is VERY important for SSH/SCP.

---

# Step 4 — Create Internet Gateway

Go to:

```
Internet Gateways → Create internet gateway
```

Name:

```
MyIGW
```

Create it.

---

# Step 5 — Attach Internet Gateway to VPC

Select:

```
MyIGW
```

Click:

```
Actions → Attach to VPC
```

Choose:

```
MyVPC
```

Attach.

---

# Step 6 — Create Route Table

Go to:

```
Route Tables → Create route table
```

Fill:

| Field | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Name | PublicRouteTable |
| VPC | MyVPC |

Create.

---

# Step 7 — Add Internet Route

Select route table.

Go to:

```
Routes → Edit routes
```

Add:

| Destination | Target |
| --- | --- |
| 0.0.0.0/0 | Internet Gateway → MyIGW |

Save.

This gives internet access.

---

# Step 8 — Associate Route Table with Subnet

Go to:

```
Subnet Associations → Edit subnet associations
```

Select:

```
PublicSubnet
```

Save.

---

# Step 9 — Create Security Group

Go to:

```
EC2 → Security Groups → Create security group
```

Fill:

| Field | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Name | MySG |
| VPC | MyVPC |

Inbound Rule:

| Type | Port | Source |
| --- | --- | --- |
| SSH | 22 | 0.0.0.0/0 |

Create.

---

# Step 10 — Launch EC2 Instance

Go to:

[AWS EC2 Console](https://console.aws.amazon.com/ec2/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Launch instance.

Under Network Settings:

| Setting | Value |
| --- | --- |
| VPC | MyVPC |
| Subnet | PublicSubnet |
| Auto Public IP | Enable |
| Security Group | MySG |

Create/download key pair.

Launch instance.

# Secure File Sharing Between Cloud Instances — COMPLETE PRACTICAL GUIDE

This guide is optimized for:

- Practical exams
- AWS EC2 Ubuntu instances
- WSL/Linux terminal
- Avoiding common errors

---

# ASSIGNMENT GOAL

You need to:

✅ Create 2 EC2 instances

✅ Connect using SSH

✅ Create file in Sender instance

✅ Transfer file using SCP

✅ Verify file in Receiver instance

---

# ARCHITECTURE

```
Your Laptop (WSL/Linux)
        ↓ SSH
Sender EC2 Instance
        ↓ SCP using Private IP
Receiver EC2 Instance
```

---

# VERY IMPORTANT UNDERSTANDING

There are 3 places where commands run.

| Place | Prompt Example |
| --- | --- |
| WSL/Linux | vivek@vivek:~$ |
| Sender EC2 | ubuntu@ip-172-31-x-x:~$ |
| Receiver EC2 | ubuntu@ip-172-31-y-y:~$ |

Always check prompt before typing commands.

---

# PART 1 — CREATE KEY PAIR

---

# STEP 1 — OPEN AWS EC2

Go to:

[AWS Console](https://console.aws.amazon.com/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Search:

```
EC2
```

Open EC2 dashboard.

---

# STEP 2 — CREATE KEY PAIR

Left sidebar:

```
Network & Security → Key Pairs
```

Click:

```
Create Key Pair
```

---

# SETTINGS

| Field | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Name | cc-practical-key |
| Type | RSA |
| Format | .pem |

Click:

```
Create Key Pair
```

File downloads automatically:

```
cc-practical-key.pem
```

---

# IMPORTANT

Keep PEM file safe.

Never share it.

This file is your login identity.

---

# PART 2 — CREATE SECURITY GROUP

---

# STEP 3 — CREATE SECURITY GROUP

Go to:

```
Security Groups
```

Click:

```
Create Security Group
```

---

# SETTINGS

| Field | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Name | cc-practical-sg |
| Description | Security group for CC practical |
| VPC | Default |

---

# ADD INBOUND RULES

Add these:

| Type | Port | Source |
| --- | --- | --- |
| SSH | 22 | 0.0.0.0/0 |

---

# WHY SSH RULE IS IMPORTANT

Without this:

❌ SSH fails

❌ SCP fails

because SCP internally uses SSH.

---

# COMMON ERROR

```
Connection timed out
```

Reason:

Port 22 blocked.

Fix:

Add SSH rule.

---

# PART 3 — CREATE SENDER INSTANCE

---

# STEP 4 — LAUNCH INSTANCE

Click:

```
Launch Instance
```

---

# SETTINGS

| Field | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Name | sender-instance |
| AMI | Ubuntu |
| Type | t2.micro |
| Key Pair | cc-practical-key |
| Security Group | cc-practical-sg |

Storage:

default 8 GB is enough.

Click:

```
Launch Instance
```

---

# IMPORTANT

Choose Ubuntu AMI.

Because Ubuntu username is:

```
ubuntu
```

NOT:

```
ec2-user
```

---

# COMMON ERROR

```
Permission denied (publickey)
```

Reason:

Wrong username.

---

# CORRECT USERNAME TABLE

| AMI | Username |
| --- | --- |
| Ubuntu | ubuntu |
| Amazon Linux | ec2-user |
| Fedora | fedora |

---

# PART 4 — CREATE RECEIVER INSTANCE

Repeat same steps.

---

# SETTINGS

| Field | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Name | receiver-instance |
| AMI | Ubuntu |
| Type | t2.micro |
| Key Pair | cc-practical-key |
| Security Group | cc-practical-sg |

Launch instance.

---

# PART 5 — IMPORTANT IP UNDERSTANDING

Each instance has:

| Type | Example |
| --- | --- |
| Public IP | 16.xx.xx.xx |
| Private IP | 172.31.xx.xx |

---

# PUBLIC IP

Used from internet.

Examples:

- SSH from laptop
- browser access

---

# PRIVATE IP

Used between EC2 instances internally.

Used for SCP between instances.

---

# MEMORY RULE

| Communication | Use Which IP |
| --- | --- |
| Laptop → EC2 | Public IP |
| EC2 → EC2 | Private IP |

---

# PART 6 — LOCAL WSL SETUP

---

# STEP 5 — OPEN WSL/LINUX TERMINAL

Prompt:

```
vivek@vivek:~$
```

---

# STEP 6 — GO TO PEM LOCATION

Example:

```bash
cd ~/CC
ls
```

You should see:

```
cc-practical-key.pem
```

---

# STEP 7 — GIVE PERMISSION TO PEM

Run:

```bash
chmod 400 cc-practical-key.pem
```

---

# WHY chmod 400?

SSH requires private key to be secure.

---

# COMMON ERROR

```
UNPROTECTED PRIVATE KEY FILE
```

Fix:

```bash
chmod 400 cc-practical-key.pem
```

---

# PART 7 — SSH INTO SENDER

---

# STEP 8 — CONNECT TO SENDER

Run in WSL:

```bash
ssh -i cc-practical-key.pem ubuntu@SENDER_PUBLIC_IP
```

Example:

```bash
ssh -i cc-practical-key.pem ubuntu@16.171.145.72
```

---

# FIRST TIME MESSAGE

You may see:

```
Are you sure you want to continue connecting?
```

Type:

```
yes
```

---

# SUCCESS

Prompt becomes:

```
ubuntu@ip-172-31-41-20:~$
```

Now you are inside Sender EC2.

---

# PART 8 — CREATE FILE

---

# STEP 9 — CREATE FILE

Run inside Sender EC2:

```bash
echo "Cloud Computing Practical" > file.txt
```

---

# STEP 10 — VERIFY FILE

```bash
ls
```

You should see:

```
file.txt
```

---

# STEP 11 — CHECK CONTENT

```bash
cat file.txt
```

Output:

```
Cloud Computing Practical
```

---

# PART 9 — COPY PEM INTO SENDER

---

# WHY THIS IS NEEDED

Sender EC2 needs PEM file to SSH into Receiver EC2.

---

# STEP 12 — EXIT SENDER

Run:

```bash
exit
```

Back to WSL.

---

# STEP 13 — COPY PEM TO SENDER

Run in WSL:

```bash
scp -i cc-practical-key.pem cc-practical-key.pem ubuntu@SENDER_PUBLIC_IP:/home/ubuntu/
```

Example:

```bash
scp -i cc-practical-key.pem cc-practical-key.pem ubuntu@16.171.145.72:/home/ubuntu/
```

---

# STEP 14 — SSH INTO SENDER AGAIN

```bash
ssh -i cc-practical-key.pem ubuntu@SENDER_PUBLIC_IP
```

---

# STEP 15 — FIX PEM PERMISSION INSIDE SENDER

Run:

```bash
chmod 400 cc-practical-key.pem
```

---

# PART 10 — TRANSFER FILE USING SCP

---

# STEP 16 — RUN SCP

IMPORTANT:

Use RECEIVER PRIVATE IP.

Run inside Sender:

```bash
scp -i cc-practical-key.pem file.txt ubuntu@RECEIVER_PRIVATE_IP:/home/ubuntu/
```

Example:

```bash
scp -i cc-practical-key.pem file.txt ubuntu@172.31.46.227:/home/ubuntu/
```

---

# EXPECTED OUTPUT

```
file.txt    100%
```

---

# COMMON ERRORS

---

# ERROR 1

```
Connection timed out
```

Reason:

Security group missing SSH rule.

Fix:

Allow port 22.

---

# ERROR 2

```
Permission denied (publickey)
```

Reason:

Wrong username.

Fix:

Use:

```
ubuntu
```

---

# ERROR 3

```
No such file or directory
```

Reason:

Wrong path or missing colon `:` in SCP.

Correct syntax:

```bash
scp file user@IP:/path
```

---

# PART 11 — VERIFY IN RECEIVER

---

# STEP 17 — EXIT SENDER

```bash
exit
```

---

# STEP 18 — SSH INTO RECEIVER

Run in WSL:

```bash
ssh -i cc-practical-key.pem ubuntu@RECEIVER_PUBLIC_IP
```

---

# STEP 19 — VERIFY FILE

Inside Receiver:

```bash
ls
```

You should see:

```
file.txt
```

---

# STEP 20 — CHECK CONTENT

```bash
cat file.txt
```

Output:

```
Cloud Computing Practical
```

